WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
♪ Somewhere over the rainbow ♪

00:00:09.143 --> 00:00:14.143
♪ Way up high ♪

00:00:16.454 --> 00:00:21.454
♪ There's a land that I heard of ♪

00:00:22.487 --> 00:00:25.487
♪ Once in a lullaby ♪

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.900
- I know at one point, Judy was called

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.300
the greatest performer of her generation,

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.550
greatest performer alive,

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.780
and Judy has this great
sense of humor in the film.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.050
And you say, "Why, is Frank Sinatra here?"

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.700
Who do you think is the
greatest performer now?

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.160
- Oh, that's good!

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.470
That's a really good question.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.303
- Thanks! (laughs)

00:00:44.303 --> 00:00:45.720
- Well there's Beyonce, yeah.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.870
- That's so funny, that's what Finn said.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:52.660
- Yeah, she just, she's, in full...

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.700
Fully access her power, at every turn.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.960
Unapologetically.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:57.973
Creatively.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.430
She's in charge.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:01.710
It's a wonderful thing to see.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.830
- So I'm wondering for you, what I loved

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.540
is that there was live
performances in this movie.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.940
I was wondering for you, how
old you were the first time

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.380
you sang a Judy song out loud,

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.810
and compare it to singing
live in this film,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.670
sort of juxtapose for me.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.820
Was it in your bedroom
with a hairbrush or?

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.013
- I'm sure that Rainbow
was in the repertoire

00:01:22.013 --> 00:01:23.150
(laughing)

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.520
- from a very early age, because you know,

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.930
I'm of the generation where
you had to be home to see it,

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.357
and every year it was on TV.

00:01:30.357 --> 00:01:31.310
"Hurry, it's on!"

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.517
You know, you run in.

00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:34.347
"Come back, the commercial's over!"

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.350
And my brother, "Shut up!" (laughs)

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.260
So, I was always singing,

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.010
and he was always asking me not to.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.400
I can't remember a specific time,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.220
but it's always been in there, yeah.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.580
- And then versus singing,
what was it like for you?

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.810
You appear, to me, to be
possessed by this character.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.540
There were so many
beautiful close-up shots,

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.300
and it was really, the
singing was amazing,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.070
but it was in the eyes that really got me.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.300
So, what was that moment like for you?

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.550
- Oh, gosh, I don't know.

00:02:06.388 --> 00:02:07.471
I don't know.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:09.683
I don't know.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.740
Just collecting information all the time,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.280
and listening to audio of her voice,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.300
and looking at videos when I
was going to sleep at night,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.180
and never being away from it, ever.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.990
- Yeah.
- It just, you know, yeah.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.720
I don't know, just felt
like a celebration,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.030
and I wanted to, you know.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.450
- Knowing that, and knowing
that you studied her

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.250
for two years, and watched
things before you went to bed,

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.720
and tried to extract every
nugget, I was wondering for you,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.020
'cause when I was
watching your performance,

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.257
I was thinking to myself,

00:02:45.257 --> 00:02:47.320
"That looks like it was exhausting."

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.610
What was it like for you when
you shot your last scene,

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:51.830
you woke up the next day,

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:53.110
and you weren't gonna play her anymore?

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.623
What was that sort of period like?

00:02:56.678 --> 00:02:58.860
- Do you know what's
funny, we didn't have that,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.447
because we finished, and it
was on to the next thing.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:04.080
"Okay, now we're gonna
record the orchestra live,"

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.207
and so "Come on, we're
gonna do the recordings

00:03:06.207 --> 00:03:09.060
"with the orchestra live for soundtrack."

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.910
And then, "Okay, we're gonna
do this other thing now,"

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:15.450
and it just kept going until
like, last month. (laughs)

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.330
- Wow!

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:17.780
And now you're gonna be talking about it

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.010
for several months.
- Right.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.320
- Do you have any Judy memorabilia?

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.840
- Yes, I do, I do.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.720
I have a couple of the
playbooks from her shows,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.190
which are (exhales), very
special, and some photographs.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:36.450
- Did you have it before
signing on to this project,

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.660
or was it as a result of doing the film?

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:40.740
- As a result.
- Yeah.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.653
- I'm not a big collector
of anything, actually.

00:03:43.653 --> 00:03:47.600
So, yeah, these were treats.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.470
- I know everybody's talking
about this performance.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.520
How do you feel when you watch it?

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.230
- I haven't seen it.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:57.230
I saw a really early cut.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.610
I haven't seen it finished yet.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.990
I might watch it this week, I don't know.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:06.810
I kinda love how I remember it,
the experience of making it.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:08.340
- Is that in general for you, you just--

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.090
- Usually, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:10.923
- Yes.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.060
Because that's real.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.400
That's your shared collaborations

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.658
with a group of people
who've become your friends,

00:04:21.658 --> 00:04:24.490
and that's what it is to me in my life,

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:27.950
and we all had the same
motivation for wanting to make it.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:30.700
We all feel affection and a
connection to her in some way,

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.170
and an appreciation for her,

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:34.750
and everybody wanted to celebrate her,

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:36.110
and that's what it felt like.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:36.943
- That's what it feels watching it.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:38.080
- Oh, thank you, I'm glad.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.690
- I know you're for kids,
you didn't talk to her kids,

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:43.910
but you said if there was
something you could've asked them,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.570
you would've asked what they
wanted to see on screen.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:47.403
- Yeah.

00:04:47.403 --> 00:04:49.840
- What do you hope people
will get and see on screen?

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.800
I'm turning the question on you.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.800
- Context, because it's
impossible to look at her life

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.750
and this period of her life, as tragic,

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.450
when you better understand
what lead to the circumstances,

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:06.630
and when you see what it is
that she was able to overcome

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.120
in order to continue
to deliver and connect

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.176
with her audiences, and the
way that just moved people

00:05:11.176 --> 00:05:15.450
for generations, it sets her apart.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.623
I mean, you can really see
how extraordinary she was.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:20.483
What?

00:05:20.483 --> 00:05:21.370
- Room service.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.847
- I didn't order anything.

00:05:22.847 --> 00:05:24.795
- There's a man under the trolley.

00:05:24.795 --> 00:05:25.628
(gasps)

00:05:25.628 --> 00:05:26.461
- Surprise!

00:05:26.461 --> 00:05:27.346
- Mickey! (laughs)

00:05:27.346 --> 00:05:28.830
(big band music)

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.254
♪ You're gonna love me ♪

00:05:30.254 --> 00:05:32.720
- The kids miss you, and
they also wanna stay put.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:33.820
- I'm coming back for them.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:34.747
- You're not listening.

00:05:34.747 --> 00:05:36.440
- I have someone I can rely on now.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.550
♪ I'm gonna love you ♪

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.597
(screaming)

00:05:41.597 --> 00:05:44.770
- Do you have to be home for
you mommy to put you to bed?

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:48.610
- There's been so much talk
about, on set, sort of like,

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.200
watching Renee, she was
like possessed by Judy.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.240
So, being there in that moment
when she was singing live,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.480
because that was one of the
beautiful things about this film

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:57.820
is she was singing live.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:58.850
Can you sort of describe it?

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:00.340
Because when they'd pan to a close-up,

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.173
you'd see it in her eyes,

00:06:01.173 --> 00:06:03.430
but being there amongst the audience.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.130
- I'll tell you that the
extras that were there

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.360
playing the audience,
all stood up every take,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.593
stood up and applauded.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.250
They weren't supposed to. (laughs)

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:15.057
The sound guy was like, "Quiet please.

00:06:15.057 --> 00:06:16.137
"We need to capture this.

00:06:16.137 --> 00:06:17.116
"Quiet."

00:06:17.116 --> 00:06:18.243
- (laughs) That's would've been me.

00:06:18.243 --> 00:06:21.070
- It was genuinely riveting.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.893
It's rare to see someone
who can, like Judy did,

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.800
be that subtle and can fill a close-up,

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.410
but can also fill a big theater,

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:37.410
and be riveting, in a
live theater experience,

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:41.030
which Renee can do.

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:43.500
- What was, for you, you
played her last husband

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:46.730
in her life, and I can't
really figure him out,

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.470
and you kinda can't figure
him out in the movie.

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:49.303
What is his motive?--

00:06:49.303 --> 00:06:50.136
- No one could figure him out.

00:06:50.136 --> 00:06:51.020
- No one can figure him out.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:51.853
Did you figure him out?

00:06:51.853 --> 00:06:53.560
How much were you able to delve into,

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.820
and was there like a fun
little nugget that you found,

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:57.063
sort of digging through?

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.180
- I found that he wrote a book,

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:06.100
which was really interesting, and in it,

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.740
they would have big blowout
fights, but at the end,

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:12.560
after this movie ends,
they got a house in London,

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:15.937
and they kinda lived there until she died.

00:07:15.937 --> 00:07:16.840
- And he found her, right, when she?

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:18.490
- Yeah, he did actually find her.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.700
He says he found her
overdosed in the bathroom.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:26.430
Knowing that it ended
happier than we ended,

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:30.430
was good for my mind,
that like he wasn't in it

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:35.160
just for himself, he was
maybe complicated and--

00:07:36.418 --> 00:07:37.251
- That's a good word for it. (laughs)

00:07:37.251 --> 00:07:40.750
- a bit self-involved in
points, but he was genuinely,

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:44.110
I think, devoted to her,
and was kind of a caretaker

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.420
at a time when she was
really needing that,

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:47.530
and was kind of flailing,

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:50.530
and had been kind of
chewed up by the system.

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:53.370
I think the fact that
he was not a movie star,

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.070
that he was kinda outside of it,

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:58.660
but was kind of a pretty reliable person,

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:02.230
and was also like youthful
and fun, and you know,

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:04.800
I think that they had
a good time together.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.010
- You got to jump out
of a room service tray,

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:07.486
how cool was that?
- Yeah, I mean, come on.

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:08.319
(laughing)

00:08:08.319 --> 00:08:09.810
Who else does that, you know?

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:11.450
No other husbands could do that.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:14.710
- Judy Garland was once called,

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:15.967
I think it was by Mickey Rooney,

00:08:15.967 --> 00:08:19.070
"The greatest performer alive."

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:19.903
- Yeah.

00:08:19.903 --> 00:08:21.380
- In your opinion, today,

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:22.630
who's the greatest performer alive.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:24.690
- Oh! That's tough.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:25.703
Ah, that's tough.

00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:29.674
I don't know.

00:08:29.674 --> 00:08:31.153
I think the difference is,

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:36.040
I can point to like great
musical performers--

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:36.873
- Go for it.
- that I've seen.

00:08:36.873 --> 00:08:39.340
- What popped in your head?

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:40.534
- Beyonce I saw live once,
- Yes!!

00:08:40.534 --> 00:08:42.750
- which is like, pretty frickin' cool.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.000
(laughing)

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.020
That Judy could do that,

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.290
'cause she could like
basically be Beyonce live,

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:53.520
but then she could like also be like

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:57.340
Kate Blanchett on screen.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.720
You know, that she was
able to do both things,

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.650
is pretty rare, you know?

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:02.483
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:09:02.483 --> 00:09:03.503
- What about an actor?

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:08.400
- You know, I saw Bryan Cranston onstage

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.120
in the show, Network, and he was able to.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.840
There was a lot of the show
that was like a projection

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:17.170
of a close-up of his face,

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:18.800
as he was talking to the audience,

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.480
so he was able to act for a close-up

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:26.300
and for Broadway theater, and
I don't know how you do that.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.143
That was pretty cool.

00:09:27.143 --> 00:09:28.154
(orchestra instrumental music)

00:09:28.154 --> 00:09:30.154
(laser)

